---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const today = [
  ['Design the bias network', 'Build the resistor divider and load resistors that set a transistor stage’s operating point, and solve the node voltages exactly.'],
  ['Model the base junction', 'A transistor’s base–emitter is a diode. Simulate that junction with a real diode model to reason about the ~0.7 V drop and where current begins to flow.'],
  ['Work the DC arithmetic', 'Supply rails, divider voltages, load lines and resistor drops all solve in DC — the numbers you need before adding the active device.'],
  ['See current and drops', 'Animated current and colour-coded node voltages make the bias conditions visible while you tune resistor values.'],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I simulate a full BJT or MOSFET amplifier right now?',
    a: 'Not as first-class parts yet. Transistors are not in the palette as complete active devices, so the simulator will not give you a full small-signal gain figure today. Transistor models are actively being expanded.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Then what is this page honestly good for?',
    a: 'For everything around the transistor: designing and solving the bias network, understanding the base–emitter diode drop, and working the DC arithmetic of a stage. That groundwork is where most beginners actually get stuck.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why model the base as a diode?',
    a: 'Because physically it is one. Using the diode model, you can see the roughly 0.7 V base–emitter drop appear and understand why base current only flows once that threshold is reached.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When will transistors become full simulatable parts?',
    a: 'Expanded transistor models are on the roadmap. Until then the biasing, diode behaviour and DC analysis you do here carry directly into transistor design once the active device lands.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Transistor circuit simulator',
    description:
      'Design and solve transistor bias networks, model the base–emitter diode, and work the DC arithmetic of a stage. Full transistor device models are being expanded.',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Transistor circuit simulator — bias networks and diode junctions"
  description="Design transistor bias networks, model the base–emitter diode, and solve the DC arithmetic of a stage in your browser. Full transistor device models are being expanded."
  path="/transistor-circuit-simulator"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 grid-paper grid-paper-fade opacity-60" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="container-page relative py-16 md:py-24">
      <p class="tnum text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">Transistor circuit simulator</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.05] tracking-tight md:text-6xl">
        Nail the bias network before the transistor goes in.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-lg text-ink-2">
        Most transistor circuits live or die on the resistors around them. Design and solve those bias
        networks here, model the base–emitter junction as the diode it is, and get the DC arithmetic
        right first.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="/simulator" class="btn btn-primary text-base">Open the simulator</a>
        <a href="/components" class="btn btn-ghost text-base">See available parts</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-page py-10">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-line-strong bg-surface p-5">
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">
        <strong class="text-ink">Straight talk:</strong> transistors are not yet full simulatable parts
        in the palette — their device models are being expanded. This page is about what genuinely works
        today: the biasing, the diode junctions and the DC groundwork of a transistor stage.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-page pb-16 md:pb-20">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">What you can do today</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {today.map(([t, d]) => (
        <div class="rounded-md border border-line bg-paper p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink">{t}</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-ink-2">{d}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm">
      <a href="/examples/voltage-divider" class="prose-link">Bias divider example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/led-resistor" class="prose-link">Diode behaviour with an LED →</a>
      <a href="/tools/ohms-law-calculator" class="prose-link">Ohm's law calculator →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface">
    <div class="container-page py-14 md:py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">Questions</h2>
      <dl class="mt-8 max-w-3xl divide-y divide-line">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="py-5">
            <dt class="font-semibold text-ink">{f.q}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 text-ink-2">{f.a}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <a href="/simulator" class="btn btn-primary mt-10 text-base">Open the simulator</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
